  "kind": "quirk",
  "title": "Text to number reads Arabic, Persian, Devanagari and Thai digits, but not fullwidth digits",
  "statement": "In the recorded en-ZA settings, converting text to a number accepts Arabic-Indic (U+0660 to U+0669), extended Arabic-Indic (U+06F0 to U+06F9), Devanagari (U+0966 to U+096F) and Thai (U+0E50 to U+0E59) digits. They work alone, in runs, with a decimal point, in the exponent, with a sign or percent, and mixed with ASCII digits or with each other: VALUE(\"1٢3\") is 123, and SQRT of a four-digit string using four scripts is the root of 1234. Fullwidth digits (U+FF10 to U+FF19) give #VALUE! alone or mixed. DATEVALUE reads the four accepted scripts in a date written year-month-day.",
  "status": "measured",
  "basis": "66 recorded VALUE readings (all ten digits of each of the five scripts, then composed forms), 10 SQRT and 4 DATEVALUE readings.",
  "inferred": "That other text-to-number routes, such as arithmetic operators, treat digits the same way is inferred from VALUE and SQRT agreeing.",
